import crate::serve::positions::position_of::position_of as to_position
import lsp_types::{Position, Range, TextEdit}

-- | The edit that makes a document canonical, or none.
-- |
-- | # One full-range edit
-- |
-- | Plan 40 decision 3. Plan 34's properties guarantee that `format`'s output
-- | is canonical, idempotent and means the same program; a minimal-diff
-- | algorithm would be new code with no property behind it, and the one thing a
-- | formatter may not do is change meaning. So the reply replaces the whole
-- | document or nothing.
-- |
-- | # Nothing, when the document is already canonical
-- |
-- | Returning an identity edit instead would work and would be worse: an editor
-- | marks a buffer dirty when it applies an edit, so format-on-save would mark
-- | every canonical file modified on every save. Plan 34 §1.5's claim that the
-- | corpus **is** the style guide is also the claim that this returns nothing
-- | for every example, and criterion 5 asserts both halves.
-- |
-- | # Nothing, when the document does not parse
-- |
-- | Plan 40's criterion 7. A formatter needs a tree, and an editor asks for
-- | formatting while the user is mid-keystroke. Returning no edit is the only
-- | honest answer — and reporting an error here would put a dialog in front of
-- | someone who merely has not finished typing.
export(crate) formatting : &str -> Option<Vec<TextEdit>>
formatting source =
  program = (.ok (redox_parser::parse_source source))?
  formatted = redox_fmt::format (&program) source
  if formatted == source
    return Some (Vec::new ())
  edit = TextEdit { range = Range { start = Position { line = 0, character = 0 }, end = to_position source (.len source) }, new_text = formatted }
  Some (vec! \-> edit)
